  "notes": [
    "Edge convention: every edge runs from a module to an object. READ means the module consumes that object; PRODUCE means it creates it. Stated because the schema does not fix a direction and a reversed dependency is exactly what a graph diff is meant to catch (R10).",
    "Built by static reading of the unit, not by tracing a run: source instrumentation is waived on record for this migration, so provenance is static. It was checked against an actual execution of the unit over 503 stand-in rows, which reached every module in this graph except the standalone-page writer and the graphical branch of the progress indicator.",
    "Three of the published objects are checkpoint objects and the others are intermediates. The intermediates are listed because a target that fuses them still agrees on every digest while losing the ability to show an analyst the parsed dates, the parsed amounts or the rendered text of one contract -- the observability loss GR-04 forbids and R10 measures.",
    "Optional at CORE tier, which this run declared. Produced anyway because the unit is one file and the graph cost minutes.",
    "The load-time module has no PRODUCE edge of its own: in the branch that fires it calls the browser-data writer, which is the module that produces the file. In the branch a clean load takes it produces nothing at all."
  ]
